# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
phaedo-opposites.md:17: argue/readings: S12 is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.2 have "opp_principle"; phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
phaedo-opposites.md:17: argue/answered: S12: answered no, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "all things which have opposites are generated out of their opposites" is false. Is S12, "And is this true of all opposites? and are we convinced that all of them are generated out of opposites?", true then?
phaedo-opposites.md:25: argue/readings: S17 is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.3 has "opp_principle"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
phaedo-opposites.md:25: argue/answered: S17: answered no, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "all things which have opposites are generated out of their opposites" is false. Is S17, "And this necessarily holds of all opposites, even though not always expressed in words--they are really generated out of one another, and there is a passing or process from one to the other of them?", true then?
phaedo-opposites.md:55: argue/readings: S36 is read 3 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 has "gen_mutual → dead_from_living"; phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.2 has "gen_mutual → dead_from_living ∧ living_from_dead"; they differ when only "life and death are generated one from the other" and "the dead are generated from the living" are true, which the first allows; phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.3 has "dead_from_living"
phaedo-opposites.md:55: argue/answered: S36: answered yes, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "life and death are generated one from the other" and "the dead are generated from the living" are true and "the living, whether things or persons, are generated from the dead" is false. Is S36, "The dead.", true then?
phaedo-opposites.md:59: argue/readings: S38 is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 has "gen_mutual → living_from_dead"; phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.2 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "life and death are generated one from the other" is true, which the second allows
phaedo-opposites.md:59: argue/answered: S38: answered no, which keeps phaedo-opposites.logic.r2, to: Suppose "life and death are generated one from the other" is true and "the living, whether things or persons, are generated from the dead" is false. Is S38, "I can only say in answer--the living.", true then?
phaedo-opposites.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 and phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.2 have "living_from_dead → souls_below"; phaedo-opposites.logic.r2.3 has "gen_mutual → dead_from_living ∧ living_from_dead" and "living_from_dead → souls_below"; they differ when only "life and death are generated one from the other" and "the dead are generated from the living" are true, which the first allows
phaedo-opposites.md:45: argue/negation: phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 P3 (S31): the sentence says "not", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "death is opposed to life, as sleep is to waking"
phaedo-opposites.md:55: argue/unfaithful: phaedo-opposites.logic.r2 P4 (S36): the sentence has no word of "life and death are generated one from the other": is it the sentence P4 came from?
